33:Previous studies on brain morphometry have reported atypical neuroanatomy in children with SeLECTS. Children with SeLECTS exhibited increasing thalamocortical structural connectivity within the motor circuit with age (Kwon et al. 2022) and showed increased regional gyrification index in the right posterior cingulate gyrus and increased sulcal depth in the right anterior cingulate gyrus and medial prefrontal cortex (Yin et al. 2024). Stratification of MRI data by age has provided evidence for variations in cortical thickness and white matter in children with SeLECTS (Smith et al. 2021). Furthermore, another study found larger gray matter volumes in the corticostriatal circuitry at the onset of epilepsy (Kim et al. 2015). The shape alterations of the putamen and caudate were also confirmed, and their relation with cognition depends on the side of epilepsy focus (Shakeri et al. 2017). While these case-control studies have detected group effects on brain morphometric measures,they do not quantify the interindividual variability that differs from case-control findings, nor do they identify potential regions that altered in a subset of children with SeLECTS. The use of normative modeling in this study compensates for limitations in these case-control studies and consider the following two points. First, the onset of children with SeLECTS often occurs during their critical period of brain development, and vulnerability in this process may be inferred from deviations from normative distributions. Second, neuroanatomical deviations may aid in monitoring possible language and cognitive dysfunction.
35:It is unknown whether morphometric measures deviations from healthy variation are present in individuals with SeLECTS and whether they are associated with clinical manifestation or cognitive function. In the current study, we employed normative modeling to estimate individual-level brain morphometric measures in children with SeLECTS. We hypothesized that children with SeLECTS would exhibit varying degrees of heterogeneity compared to the normal population. To test these hypotheses, we utilized the CentileBrain framework to derive normative model parameters based on the developing Chinese Color Nest Project (depCCNP) dataset and subsequently estimated deviations in brain morphometric measures to characterize the differences between children with SeLECTS and the normative range. Finally, we examined whether regional deviations in brain morphometric measures from the normative range were associated with clinical manifestation or cognitive function.
41:The current work encompassed two independent cohorts: a casecontrol cohort and a normal sample for model training. The casecontrol cohort comprised 187 children with SeLECTS, including 98 boys and 89 girls, aged 4 to 18 years, with a mean age of 9.09 ± 2.66 years (mean ± SD). Additionally, the study included 108 controls, consisting of 61 boys and 47 girls, aged 4 to 15 years, with a mean age of 9.42 ± 2.49 years (Table 1). Epilepsy diagnoses were confirmed by two experienced neurologists, adhering to the International League against Epilepsy (ILAE) classifications (Berg et al. 2010). All participants had negative presentations on conventional (structural) MRI. Cognitive psychological assessments, such as Raven's Standard Progressive Matrices and the Integrated Visual and Auditory Continuous Performance Test, were administered to 75 children with SeLECTS. Raven's Progressive Matrices is a nonverbal test typically used to measure analog perception, reasoning, and abstract abilities and is regarded as a nonverbal estimate of fluid intelligence. The Integrated Visual and Auditory
45:To ensure the generalizability of the normative models, we included additional normal data to train the models. The data for model training consists of two samples: the CKG sample and the PEK sample. Samples from the two different centers are both sourced from the developing Chinese Color Nest Project (devCCNP). The devCCNP resource is accessible within the Chinese Color Nest Project (CCNP)-Lifespan Brain-Mind Development Data Community (https://ccnp.scidb.cn) hosted by the Science Data Bank. The two samples are combined to train the normative models, and the potential impact of site on performance is addressed by treating site as a random factor and using ComBatGAM (Pomponio et al. 2020) for site harmonization. These normal data comprise two samples: the CKG sample and the PEK sample. The two samples were combined to train the normative models, and both originate. This project enrolled typically developing school-age Chinese children and adolescents, and this study comprised 241 boys and 216 girls, aged 3 to 18 years, with a mean age of 10.44 ± 3.11 years. All normal participants had no history of neurological disorder or psychiatric illness and no gross abnormalities on brain MRI images. This project was approved by the Institutional Review Board of the Institute of Psychology, Chinese Academy of Sciences. Written informed consent was obtained from one of the participants' legal guardians, and written assent was obtained from the participants.
49:Structural images were acquired on a Siemens Trio Tim 3.0tesla MRI scanner at Jinling Hospital. We used foam padding to minimize head motion. All participants were required to keep their eyes closed and to keep their heads still. Highresolution T1-weighted MRI data were acquired in a sagittal orientation using a magnetization-prepared rapid gradientecho sequence (repetition time/echo time=2,300/2.98 ms, flip angle=9 ◦ , field of view=256 × 256 mm 2 , matrix size = 256 × 256, slice thickness=1 mm, no interslice gap, and 176 slices). We also used T1-weighted MRI data provided by the devCCNP. Full details for the devCCNP have been published (Fan et al. 2023).
57:To ensure generalizability and robustness, we followed the normative modeling framework provided by CentileBrain (Ge et al. 2024). Using age and sex as covariates, normative models for each regional morphometric measure were developed utilizing
74:data from a multisite sample of 457 healthy individuals (47.3% female; aged 3 to 18 years) from the devCCNP. These data were used to construct the models, primarily based on the following considerations. First, while the original CentileBrain model encompasses a lifespan from 3 to 90 years, our study specifically focuses on the distribution of brain morphology during children's development (3 to 18 years). Secondly, although the original CentileBrain collected samples from diverse regions and ethnicities globally, our subsequent analysis of childhood epilepsy predominantly centered on the Chinese population. Leveraging the CentileBrain pipeline, we opted for multivariable fractional polynomial regression implemented with the mfp package in R. This algorithm can determine the functional form of the predictor by testing a wide family of shapes and multiple turning points while providing a good fit at the extremes of the covariates. The optimal covariate combination included site harmonization (ComBat-GAM) ( Pomponio et al. 2020) and the incorporation of intracranial volume, mean cortical thickness, and mean surface area in the regional models for subcortical volume, cortical thickness, and surface area regional measures, respectively.
78:The sex-specific and region-specific model parameters by the CentileBrain normative modeling framework were subsequently applied to each region morphometric measure of the children with SeLECTS and healthy controls. The normative deviation for each regional morphometric measure was defined as the z -scores:
82:where Y represents the observed value and ˆ Y represents the predicted value. A positive or negative z -score signifies that the predicted value of the corresponding morphometric measurement lies above or below the normative mean, respectively. In accordance with previous literature (ENIGMA Clinical High Risk for Psychosis Working Group et al. 2024), we defined regional nonnormal z -scores as infra-normal when they fell below -1.96 or supra-normal when they exceeded 1.96, corresponding to the 5th percentile and 95th percentile, respectively. The median range ( z -scores between -1.96 and 1.96) were classified as within the normal range. For each morphometric measure, we determined the percentage of participants with infra-normal or supra-normal deviations for each region, calculated the percentage of participants exhibiting infra- or supra-normal deviations across varying numbers of regions, and compared these percentages between the SeLECTS and control groups. To gain further insight into the spatial pattern of brain morphology development in children with
